# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cadgui-action-layer — one Action Layer so the app is AI-driveable AND testable
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: cadgui-2-qt6-gui-host
#+TARGET: release 3.0
#+BRANCH: qt6-gui
#+DATE: 2026-11-16
#+PARENT: cadgui-release-3.0-epic

* Context

Cross-cutting subgoal of [[cadgui-release-3.0-epic]]. Scripting, Lisp and the
=-command= CLI already make the ENGINE AI-driveable. Extend that to the WHOLE
application: every user-facing interaction -- menus, ribbon, dialogs, palettes,
grips, osnap, canvas picks, mouse modes, sysvar changes -- invokable AND
introspectable through ONE API, at a human's fidelity. The GUI must be a THIN
presentation over a named, typed, documented action layer; nothing reaches
application state except through it. The point: the layer a TEST harness drives
and the layer an AGENT drives are the SAME layer -- so making the app testable
and making it AI-operable are one piece of work.

This is why it is in the epic: it is the substrate that makes the epic's
"implement -> test -> document every slice" methodology enforceable.

* Design: one Action Layer, four surfaces

1. *Central action registry.* Every action -- command, menu item, ribbon button,
   dialog control, grip op, canvas interaction mode, sysvar set -- registered
   ONCE: stable id, typed params, docstring, category, enabled/disabled predicate,
   keybinding / menu path. Generalises cador's =*cador-commands*= table and the
   DCL tile protocol into one surface.
2. *Uniform dispatch.* ONE entry point -- =(invoke-action ID typed-args...)= --
   called identically by the GUI, the =-command= CLI, the script/AutoLISP layer
   and the AI-driver API. The GUI is one caller; no widget path mutates state by
   any other route.
3. *Introspection API.* Query the registry and LIVE state as structured data
   (s-expr / JSON): =list-actions=, =describe-action= (params/types/doc/enabled),
   =get-state= (active command + PENDING PROMPT and its expected input type,
   selection set, sysvars, open dialogs + fields/values, view/layers). The AI
   discovers what is possible and the current state WITHOUT screen-scraping.
4. *Structured interaction protocol.* When a command needs input the app EMITS a
   typed request ("need a POINT", "need an ENTITY matching X", "dialog FIELD
   expects Y", "pick one of KEYWORDS"); the driver answers with a typed value;
   the app streams structured EVENTS of what it did. Generalises cador's
   prompt-api seam and the alfe file-protocol / DCL s-expr wire to dialogs,
   canvas picks and menus.

* The feature that ENSURES it (the driveability guard)

A CI check -- same discipline as the change-gating completeness guard
(=scripts/ci-classify-changes.py=) -- FAILS the build unless EVERY action is:
(a) registered with a stable id, typed params and a docstring; (b) reachable by
=invoke-action= with NO GUI back-door -- verified by enumerating every GUI signal
binding and asserting each dispatches a registered action, not a direct
mutation; and (c) covered by at least one HEADLESS test that drives it through
the API offscreen. A new ribbon button with no action-id, an action with no
doc/types, or a widget touching the model directly -> red. Turns "the whole app
is AI-driveable and introspectable" from a hope into a CHECKED INVARIANT.

* Record / replay

Every session records as a transcript of =(invoke-action + supplied inputs)=,
replayable deterministically offscreen. That transcript IS the test fixture, the
AI's feedback loop, and the bug repro -- one artefact for tests, agents and users.

* Buildable slices

- *A. Registry + dispatch.* Define the action record + =invoke-action=; register
  cador's existing commands through it (adapter over =*cador-commands*=). TEST:
  fiveam -- register, describe, invoke; a known command runs via =invoke-action=.
- *B. Introspection.* =list-actions= / =describe-action= / =get-state= returning
  structured data. TEST: snapshot assertions on a scripted session's state.
- *C. Structured interaction.* The typed request/answer + event stream over the
  cadgui input bridge (phase 2's nested loop). TEST: a scripted driver answers a
  POINT request and reads the resulting entity-created event.
- *D. The driveability guard.* The CI check (a/b/c above), wired like
  =check-ci-classify-changes=. TEST: the guard itself (a deliberately
  unregistered action -> red; a registered+tested one -> green).
- *E. Record/replay.* Transcript capture + headless replay. TEST: replay a
  recorded transcript, assert identical final state.

* Status

- DONE (2026-11-17, 2.4.27): slice *A (registry + dispatch)* and most of *B
  (introspection)* -- =clautolisp/cadgui= module =cadgui/source/actions.lisp=.
  =*cadgui-actions*= registry (id -> typed-params + doc + category + fn);
  =cadgui-register-action=; the single dispatch entry point
  =cadgui-invoke-action= (signals =cadgui-unknown-action= on an unknown id);
  introspection =cadgui-list-actions= (optional category filter) +
  =cadgui-describe-action= (a (:id :category :doc :params) plist). The existing
  cadgui operations are registered as built-in actions (:add-line, :add-circle,
  :eval-command, :entity-count, :selection). Covered by cadgui-suite tests
  (actions-tests.lisp): list/describe the built-ins, invoke draws through the
  single entry point, an unknown action signals, re-registration replaces.
- NEXT in this issue: =get-state= (active command + pending prompt + selection +
  sysvars + view) to finish *B*; adapter over cador's =*cador-commands*= so
  every command is an action; *C* structured interaction (typed request/answer
  + event stream over the cadgui input bridge); *D* the driveability guard (CI
  check: every action registered + reachable only via =invoke-action= + headless
  test); *E* record/replay. The GUI command line already routes AutoLISP through
  the =:eval-command= action's implementation; wiring widgets exclusively
  through =invoke-action= lands with *D*.

* Where it sits, what it reuses

Established WITH phase 2 (not a late add-on), so every later phase registers its
actions through the same layer. Phase 4's CRX SDK registers its commands /
custom entities through the SAME registry ([[cadgui-4-crx-api]]). Reuses the
=*cador-commands*= registry, the prompt-api input seam, the DCL s-expr wire and
the alfe protocol as seeds.

* Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every cadgui canvas interaction + command is a registered action.
- [ ] GUI widgets mutate state ONLY via =invoke-action= (guard enforces).
- [ ] =list-actions= / =describe-action= / =get-state= return structured data.
- [ ] A scripted AI-style driver runs a command to completion via the typed
      request/answer protocol, headless.
- [ ] The driveability guard is green in CI and fails on a back-door.
- [ ] A recorded session replays to the identical final state.
